00:03:49.150 --> 00:03:52.660
Well, let's sort of start at the
most basic level that we can.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.379
What is a virtual machine?

00:03:54.379 --> 00:03:55.920
Like, I'm sure a
lot of our listeners

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.890
probably have a bit
of an idea, but I

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:00.340
think let's sort of go-- let's
go as simple as possible.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.300
What is it?

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:02.425
SCOTT VAN WOUDENBERG: Yeah.

00:04:02.425 --> 00:04:05.910
So this is actually
kind of a tough question

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:09.460
to answer tersely, I
guess, or succinctly.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:11.360
I spent a little time
kind of researching,

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:14.570
and took a look at what
Wikipedia had to say about it.

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:17.120
And kind of the most basic
definition I could find

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:18.950
was essentially
a virtual machine

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:23.890
is a software implementation of
a physical computer or server.

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:26.240
And then obviously
on Wikipedia it

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:28.620
was followed by a
few pages of fairly

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.860
low-level technical
detail on virtualization,

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:33.700
on hypervisors, on
paravirtualized devices,

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:36.400
on-- the list goes on and on.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:41.344
And then along with
about two dozen reference

00:04:41.344 --> 00:04:43.010
links down at the
bottom where you could

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:45.250
go read about more detail.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.780
So I kind of stepped back
and thought about it.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:50.320
And I think for the
purposes of this discussion

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.240
and just kind of for
cloud customers that

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:56.950
are looking at GCE or
comparing it against EC2

00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:58.930
or just trying to
understand, maybe you

00:04:58.930 --> 00:05:00.740
come from an App
Engine background

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:04.496
or you're a front-end
developer or-- depending

00:05:04.496 --> 00:05:05.870
on where you're
coming from, it's

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.060
best just to kind think
of a VM as a trusted

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.770
ubiquitous computing
environment that

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:13.670
lets you decouple
the actual code that

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:17.100
runs from the hardware
that it runs on.

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:18.990
So think of it as
an abstraction layer

00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:22.290
that is both secure
and ubiquitous,

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.810
which are kind of
the two things that

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:28.700
have made virtual machines
as popular as they are.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:30.672
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
So like containers?

00:05:30.672 --> 00:05:32.130
SCOTT VAN WOUDENBERG:
So containers

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:35.680
is actually a very-- that
is an excellent example

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.970
of a computing abstraction
that is not yet very secure.

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:44.490
And it is also not yet quite as
ubiquitous as virtual machines.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:46.930
There will be a day
sometime down the road

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:49.530
where all of the security
issues get worked out,

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.140
like you can have complete
isolation between two

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:54.340
containers running
on the same host

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:59.090
and there'll be a very
secure, trusted sandbox

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:01.360
is kind of how we think
about these environments.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.270
Computing abstraction layers
run in these sandboxes,

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:08.070
kind of like a JVM, kind of
like any interpreted language

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:10.360
has like a runtime environment.

00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:12.640
And you know, that runtime
environment, that sandbox,

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:17.430
has to be proven to be
secure and impossible for one

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:20.170
container to break out of
that sandbox environment

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:25.195
to muck with the memory or other
area-- a different container's

00:06:25.195 --> 00:06:26.570
environment, so
that you can have

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:30.820
a true multi-tenant
computing platform, which

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:32.680
is really what VMs gives you.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:34.230
And the reason why
VMs is where it is

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:39.280
is because VMware started this
whole game 10, 12 years ago,

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.850
and has basically taken
the market by storm.

00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:47.380
Back in the day before VMware,
everything ran just directly

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:47.940
on hardware.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:50.170
And VMware added this
virtualization abstraction,

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:51.470
and it really took off.

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:53.852
But you know, it's
been secure-- they've

00:06:53.852 --> 00:06:55.560
been finding all the
bugs and making sure

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.510
that it's secure for
the last 10, 12 years.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:07:00.220
So containers is just kind of
at the beginning of that curve,

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:01.040
I think.

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.010
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
[INAUDIBLE] security

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:04.480
concerns that you
were mentioning,

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:09.350
why could someone choose running
a VM using Google Compute

00:07:09.350 --> 00:07:12.355
Engine rather than App Engine or
Kubernetes on Container Engine?

00:07:12.355 --> 00:07:13.480
SCOTT VAN WOUDENBERG: Yeah.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:19.340
So I mean, the simplest answer
is because that's what-- like,

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:21.630
enterprise is anybody
coming to the cloud that

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:25.480
has existing workloads
either on prem or on AWS

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:28.330
or elsewhere, it's going
to be-- like a Compute

00:07:28.330 --> 00:07:30.800
Engine or Infrastructure
as a Service is going

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:35.101
to be the most familiar
and probably most

00:07:35.101 --> 00:07:36.600
like what they're
currently running,

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.680
especially enterprises
where most of them

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:42.539
are running on-- in their
data centers or in colo

00:07:42.539 --> 00:07:43.580
environments or whatever.

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:45.037
They're probably using VMware.

00:07:45.037 --> 00:07:46.620
The majority of them
are using VMware,

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:48.050
running virtual machines.

00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:49.383
It's what they're familiar with.

00:07:49.383 --> 00:07:51.300
This is basically
infrastructure is

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:55.100
going to be most customers'
on-ramp into the cloud.

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:56.760
And then once
they're on the cloud

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:59.410
and they're out of the business
of managing their own data

00:07:59.410 --> 00:08:03.800
centers, like doing their three-
or five-year hardware refreshes

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:05.990
and dealing with all
that stuff, once they've

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:08.980
moved those applications
to the cloud,

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:11.110
onto GCE, from
there they'll then

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:13.861
be able to start
decomposing, rewriting,

00:08:13.861 --> 00:08:15.860
rewriting components,
kind of splitting them out

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:17.700
into microservices
and taking advantage

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.180
of some of the other
really cool technologies

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:21.750
the cloud platform offers.

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:23.820
And not-- our cloud
platform obviously

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:26.560
offers some really amazing--
especially big data analytics,

00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:30.010
machine learning, we've got a
ton of really amazing products

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:32.850
that abstract-- that
are built at a higher

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:33.820
level of abstraction.

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:36.711
Like, the machine learning is
basically an open source API,

00:08:36.711 --> 00:08:38.669
so you don't actually
have to manage underlying

00:08:38.669 --> 00:08:41.230
resources if you don't want to.

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:43.730
And Kubernetes is a great
example of that, where you just

00:08:43.730 --> 00:08:47.837
write your code and write your
containers, like what you want

00:08:47.837 --> 00:08:49.420
to implement in a
container, deploy it

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:51.600
as a microservice and
deploy dozens of other ones,

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:53.520
and they all start
talking to each other.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:55.280
That's really kind
of the power there.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:57.430
You don't have to think
about the hardware.

00:08:57.430 --> 00:08:59.290
How much RAM, how many cores?

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:01.225
Like, am I using it efficiently?

00:09:01.225 --> 00:09:02.100
All that other stuff.

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:03.230
You just stop thinking about it.

00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:05.230
And that's the advantage
of moving up the stack.

00:09:05.230 --> 00:09:10.120
But as far as getting workloads
into cloud, the first step,

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.150
the on-ramp is going to be GCE.

00:09:12.150 --> 00:09:14.350
MARK MANDEL: Apart from
that sort of general on-ramp

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:16.970
to come into the cloud, are
there any particular use cases

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:18.715
you see that people
are like, oh,

00:09:18.715 --> 00:09:20.090
VMs are just much
better for this

00:09:20.090 --> 00:09:21.470
than, say, maybe
some of the more

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:23.080
shiny technologies out there?

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:27.340
SCOTT VAN WOUDENBERG:
So I think it really

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:30.010
kind of depends on what
people like to focus on.

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:32.010
At the end of the
day, virtual machines

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:35.580
give you absolute
control over every layer.

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:38.280
Every piece of the
software, you control.

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:40.940
You are the one that writes
all the code to do, like,

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:45.690
auto-scaling up and down,
and basically scaling

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:47.360
the number of VMs
that you have based

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:49.450
on the incoming
demand and capa--

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:53.260
and you know, requests per
second, whatever the metric is.

00:09:53.260 --> 00:09:57.460
So really, for the very
sophisticated customers

00:09:57.460 --> 00:10:00.020
or applications that
need control over

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:01.824
every aspect of
their application,

00:10:01.824 --> 00:10:03.240
virtual machines
is probably going

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:07.780
to continue to be the go-to
product or go-to technology

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:09.520
for some of those.

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:13.630
There's also stuff like if you
have to run something like SQL

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:17.190
Server or something like a
third-party software package

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:21.340
that doesn't really have
a containerized version,

00:10:21.340 --> 00:10:25.530
or that doesn't-- it's not
available to an App Engine app,

00:10:25.530 --> 00:10:27.960
like a video encoding
library, for example.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:30.140
Like, we have tons of
App Engine customers

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:33.550
that basically use App
Engine to orchestrate--

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:35.990
to do the front-end
serving, but then also

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:39.080
to orchestrate like kind
of transcoding workloads,

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:42.212
like media rendering,
that sort of thing.

00:10:42.212 --> 00:10:44.670
And that's where they basically
break out of the App Engine

00:10:44.670 --> 00:10:50.680
layer, use GAE, App
Engine Flex to run GCE VMs

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:52.240
but still manage my App Engine.

00:10:52.240 --> 00:10:54.610
And those VMs, the only
thing that they're doing

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:59.130
is pulling videos
or audio off of SQ

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:02.070
and transcoding it, because
you can actually run

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:05.730
lib mmpeg and the other
transcoding libraries,

00:11:05.730 --> 00:11:07.611
which are typically
written in C.

00:11:07.611 --> 00:11:09.860
You can just run those in a
VM, where you can't really

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:12.110
run those in App Engine
without rewriting them

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:14.740
as a Python library or
something like that,

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:16.064
which is just too much work.

00:11:16.064 --> 00:11:17.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I think
you just went exactly

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:21.910
through the architecture of
[INAUDIBLE] presented at Google

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:23.630
[INAUDIBLE] was exactly that.

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:24.190
SCOTT VAN WOUDENBERG: Yep.

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:24.689
Yep.

00:11:24.689 --> 00:11:27.460
I mean, it's a very
popular, very popular model.

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:31.000
And App Engine makes it really--
App Engine plus G App Engine

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.290
Flex make it really
easy to manage the VMs.

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:36.070
You still-- you know
that they're there,

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:38.159
but it handles all
of the orchestration

00:11:38.159 --> 00:11:39.200
and so you don't have to.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:41.030
And it just makes
it super simple.

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:43.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I have
one more question regarding

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:46.230
this trend towards containers.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:49.920
So you said that, for VMs, it's
like a very good place to when

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:53.570
you're doing my favorite
phrase, lift and shift,

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:54.940
to get started on the cloud.

00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:57.060
Then some people
move to containers.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:00.560
And what is your
opinion on this trend

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:03.880
towards serverless computing,
taking into account

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:06.840
that all your business
is selling servers?

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.490
SCOTT VAN WOUDENBERG: So
serverless is-- I mean,

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:11.210
there's a couple
interpretations.

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:13.660
So one interpretation
of what that means

00:12:13.660 --> 00:12:16.000
is I just write code.

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:19.240
I don't have to think about
the underlying deployment

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:19.980
orchestration.

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:23.270
I don't have to think
about how that code gets

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:26.870
scaled up and scaled down
to handle increased load,

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:29.520
like the diurnal cycles,
that sort of thing.

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:32.700
So it basically just
means that the product

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:34.540
or whatever it is
that I'm programming

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:39.220
against has abstracted away
the fact that under the covers

00:12:39.220 --> 00:12:41.350
there's servers that are
connected over networks

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:42.570
and stuff like that.

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:45.450
So that's kind of
one interpretation.

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:47.150
Another one is
just, look, I just

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:51.380
have kind of like the cloud
functions model or the AWS

00:12:51.380 --> 00:12:54.890
lambda model, where you're just
writing these little snippets

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:59.000
of code that you need to run
when some trigger comes in,

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:02.660
like-- I don't
know, like I've just

00:13:02.660 --> 00:13:06.240
pushed a new request onto--
like an entry onto a task queue.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:09.960
I want that to trigger a cloud
function to run and pull that

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.710
off the task queue and
do something to it,

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:13.501
and then push it back
onto a different task

00:13:13.501 --> 00:13:14.320
queue or something.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:17.890
And that's really where
something like cloud functions,

00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:20.807
you really aren't even--
you're worried about even less.

00:13:20.807 --> 00:13:22.390
You just write a
function, essentially

00:13:22.390 --> 00:13:24.930
what ends up being like
a function in whatever

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:26.450
your favorite language is.

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:29.450
And then you hook it up--
you set up a trigger,

00:13:29.450 --> 00:13:32.040
and then that function goes
off and does something which

00:13:32.040 --> 00:13:35.690
may trigger other functions
to run, which gives you

00:13:35.690 --> 00:13:38.452
this ability to kind of just
write individual functions,

00:13:38.452 --> 00:13:40.410
hook them all together,
and you end up building

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:43.280
like these kind of crazy
complicated applications

00:13:43.280 --> 00:13:46.780
that never have
to touch and never

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:51.610
have to even think about the
notion of even a container.

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:54.244
Like, it's completely abstracted
away even beyond that.

00:13:54.244 --> 00:13:55.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:58.990
So since we're talking
about Compute Engine

00:13:58.990 --> 00:14:01.720
and you are pretty much
the expert on the topic,

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:05.550
what are your favorite
features of GCE?

00:14:05.550 --> 00:14:08.940
SCOTT VAN WOUDENBERG:
So I think like a lot

00:14:08.940 --> 00:14:10.680
of my favorite
features come from kind

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:12.617
of the underlying
flexible infrastructure

00:14:12.617 --> 00:14:13.450
that we're built on.

00:14:13.450 --> 00:14:18.050
So two of the best examples,
and my personal favorites,

00:14:18.050 --> 00:14:20.380
are the permanent
billing that we have,

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:22.910
and then the automatic
sustained use discounts.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:26.210
So permanent billing, you know,
most of the big hyperscale

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:30.644
cloud offerings right
now bill per hour.

00:14:30.644 --> 00:14:33.060
So if you spin up a virtual
machine, you run it for, like,

00:14:33.060 --> 00:14:34.970
20 minutes, and then
you shut it down,

00:14:34.970 --> 00:14:37.520
you get charged
for an entire hour.

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:40.510
On Compute Engine, you only get
charged for those 20 minutes.

00:14:40.510 --> 00:14:44.110
And then if you start it up
again and run it for another 15

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:46.630
minutes, then you only get
charged for those 15 minutes,

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:48.560
instead of it being
rounded up to an hour.

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:50.575
Now, we do have a
10-minute minimum.

00:14:50.575 --> 00:14:52.200
So if you spin it up
for three minutes,

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:53.491
you get charged for 10 minutes.

00:14:53.491 --> 00:14:59.130
But really, that's-- it's pretty
significant savings if what

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:02.900
you're doing with the VM doesn't
typically take on the order

00:15:02.900 --> 00:15:04.530
of multiple hours.

00:15:04.530 --> 00:15:09.550
And obviously, the longer the
VM runs, the less of an impact--

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:11.810
or less of a savings you get.

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:13.920
But that's where sustained
use discounts comes in,

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:17.520
which is the longer you run
the VM for a given month,

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:20.620
the cheaper that VM-- the
per hour, or per minute cost

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:21.780
of that VM is.

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:25.370
So if you run a VM 24/7
for an entire month,

00:15:25.370 --> 00:15:27.990
you basically get,
like, 30% discount off

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:30.314
of the list price for
that entire month.

00:15:30.314 --> 00:15:32.230
And we just kind of--
at the end of the month,

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:36.770
we aggregate all of your
VMs up and do the math

00:15:36.770 --> 00:15:38.660
to basically give you
a discount over all

00:15:38.660 --> 00:15:41.310
of the usage based on how long
those VMs ran for that month.

00:15:41.310 --> 00:15:45.066
And that's all-- especially
the permanent billing, that's

00:15:45.066 --> 00:15:46.440
really kind of
comes down to like

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:49.800
some of the flexible real-time
data processing infrastructure

00:15:49.800 --> 00:15:51.730
that we have here
at Google that lets

00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:55.790
us do near real-time
billing and monetization

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:58.280
like that, which is--
when you're talking

00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:03.450
about the number of VMs
that GCE runs on behalf

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.400
of their customers, like doing
per minute billing for all

00:16:06.400 --> 00:16:08.840
of those VMs all the time is
actually a pretty significant

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:09.924
data processing challenge.

00:16:09.924 --> 00:16:12.090
But that's the type of thing
that Google is good at,

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:14.710
and so we've leveraged some
of that in-house expertise

00:16:14.710 --> 00:16:16.650
to solve it.

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:20.930
Another of my favorite features
is custom machine types.

00:16:20.930 --> 00:16:26.120
That's basically when you go
to create a VM, you typically

00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:28.560
specify what's called an
instance type or a machine

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:29.200
type.

00:16:29.200 --> 00:16:32.920
And that basically is
kind of the hardware spec,

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:34.850
like the virtual
hardware spec of the VM.

00:16:34.850 --> 00:16:39.010
So it's the number of cores you
want, how much RAM you want.

00:16:39.010 --> 00:16:41.960
And basically what
GCE offers that none

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:44.310
of the major competitors
offer is the ability

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:45.440
to customize that.

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:50.740
Like you know what, I
don't need eight cores.

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:52.880
But four cores is
too small, so I'm

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:54.560
going to create a six-core VM.

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:57.050
And I happen to know that
I need two gigs per core,

00:16:57.050 --> 00:16:59.600
so I'm going to create a
six-core, 12 gigabyte of RAM

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:02.250
virtual machine using
custom machine types.

00:17:02.250 --> 00:17:06.730
And that lets me save money
over the next highest up.

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:09.166
So actually, let me
take a quick step back.

00:17:09.166 --> 00:17:12.079
Like, we also--
like GCE, as well as

00:17:12.079 --> 00:17:15.220
the other major public
clouds, give you

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:17.520
a selection of predefined
instance types or machine

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:18.020
types.

00:17:18.020 --> 00:17:21.310
So Google calls them,
like, N1 standard 1,

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:23.470
N1 high mem4, et cetera.

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:25.109
And the standard
high mem high CPU

00:17:25.109 --> 00:17:29.910
really just refers to
the RAM-to-core ratio.

00:17:29.910 --> 00:17:32.150
Competitors offer--
like AWS offers

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:35.760
the C34x large, C42x large,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:40.070
So there's these predefined
machine types that you get,

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:42.130
or that you have to choose from.

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:44.780
But if your workload
doesn't fit exactly

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:47.220
into-- like it's
too small for, like,

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:52.250
a C42x large but too small
for a C4x large-- or too big

00:17:52.250 --> 00:17:54.980
for a C4x large, there's
no-- you basically

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:57.130
have to buy the
next size up, or you

00:17:57.130 --> 00:18:00.500
have to figure out how to
tailor your workload so

00:18:00.500 --> 00:18:03.100
that it fits exactly into one
of those predefined shapes.

00:18:03.100 --> 00:18:05.790
And that's where custom
machine types really shines,

00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:09.060
is the ability to tailor the
VM shape to the workload,

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:09.822
not vice versa.

00:18:09.822 --> 00:18:11.780
And that's especially
important for enterprises

00:18:11.780 --> 00:18:14.040
coming to the cloud,
because they've probably--

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:16.900
what they're probably running on
is, like, a Dell server that's

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:18.980
got some number of cores
and some amount of RAM,

00:18:18.980 --> 00:18:22.590
and that's basically their
homogeneous fleet that they've

00:18:22.590 --> 00:18:24.290
run-- they're running
right now for all

00:18:24.290 --> 00:18:25.700
these different applications.

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:28.140
And instead of having to
kind of scratch their heads

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:31.410
and figure out how to adjust
the workload so that it fits

00:18:31.410 --> 00:18:33.040
in with one of these
predefined types,

00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:34.540
they just go create
a custom machine

00:18:34.540 --> 00:18:37.050
type that exactly mimics
what they have on prem,

00:18:37.050 --> 00:18:38.610
and they're off to the races.

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:41.870
It's one less kind of
pain point or friction

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:45.521
point in moving to GCE from an
existing on-prem environment.

00:18:45.521 --> 00:18:48.020
MARK MANDEL: And that would
seem to me as well like a really

00:18:48.020 --> 00:18:49.170
nice cost savings, as well.

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:51.620
I know-- I do some stuff
with gaming companies,

00:18:51.620 --> 00:18:54.297
and so often they want, like,
necessarily a whole lot of RAM,

00:18:54.297 --> 00:18:55.880
but they don't
necessarily need, like,

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:57.347
the fastest CPU in the world.

00:18:57.347 --> 00:18:59.430
So when you're in that
sort of cookie-cutter world

00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:01.410
and you have to have both
of those at the same time,

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:03.070
this is a nice way
to say, hey, no, we

00:19:03.070 --> 00:19:05.500
don't need that much CPU,
that's fine, but lots of RAM.

00:19:05.500 --> 00:19:07.460
So we can really
tailor-- spend our money

00:19:07.460 --> 00:19:08.460
exactly where we need.

00:19:08.460 --> 00:19:08.920
SCOTT VAN WOUDENBERG: Yes.

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:09.570
Exactly.

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:09.840
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:12.381
There's-- one of the things--
you've been talking a lot about

00:19:12.381 --> 00:19:14.810
the flexibility of
our infrastructure.

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:17.250
And one of the places
where I think it shows

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:19.990
is the fact that we're
able to [? reside ?]

00:19:19.990 --> 00:19:24.600
precise persistent disks
while the instance is running

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:26.030
and the disk it attached to it.

00:19:26.030 --> 00:19:26.770
SCOTT VAN WOUDENBERG: Yep.

00:19:26.770 --> 00:19:27.350
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is something

00:19:27.350 --> 00:19:29.460
that, the first time I
showed to a customer,

00:19:29.460 --> 00:19:31.415
they were just
impressed with that.

00:19:31.415 --> 00:19:32.970
It was like, is this magic?

00:19:32.970 --> 00:19:34.305
How does that work?

00:19:34.305 --> 00:19:35.430
SCOTT VAN WOUDENBERG: Yeah.

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:36.204
That's funny.

00:19:36.204 --> 00:19:38.370
That was something that we
launched fairly recently,

00:19:38.370 --> 00:19:44.000
and I don't think it's-- don't
think it's really kind of hit

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:48.410
the kind of customer psyche,
or whatever you want to-- kind

00:19:48.410 --> 00:19:51.120
of collective customer psyche,
because it is a super powerful

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:51.920
feature.

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:54.410
And the customers that
have encountered-- you

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:55.910
know, there's a
lot of issues that

00:19:55.910 --> 00:20:00.490
happen that could cause your--
like especially like your root

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:03.390
boot partition to fill
up, like your boot PD.

00:20:03.390 --> 00:20:05.170
Like typically, we
only allocate, like,

00:20:05.170 --> 00:20:06.840
10 gigs' worth or
20 gigs' worth.

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:09.340
You keep those small, because
you don't put any data on them

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:10.173
and stuff like that.

00:20:10.173 --> 00:20:13.240
But still, any-- most
guest OS dump, like,

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:16.470
all of their logging
information on there, et cetera.

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:18.950
And so if, for whatever reason,
you're not paying attention

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:20.450
and you don't have
any alerts set up

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:22.320
and the root volume
fills up with, like,

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:25.350
log data or something,
then all of a sudden

00:20:25.350 --> 00:20:28.970
you're now no longer able
to SSH it into your VM,

00:20:28.970 --> 00:20:32.620
because SSH relies on being
able to write its own logs.

00:20:32.620 --> 00:20:35.590
And when that fails, like
everything fails, basically.

00:20:35.590 --> 00:20:37.855
And this is like common--
not just on-- this

00:20:37.855 --> 00:20:42.120
is basically any Linux,
any server that runs.

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:45.360
If you fill up the root
partition, bad things happen.

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:49.230
And this hot resize is just
a super easy and quick way

00:20:49.230 --> 00:20:52.340
to be like, oh crap,
I had a runaway log

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:54.450
script or something, and
my volume's filled up.

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:58.000
Let me just go into the
UI, click two buttons,

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:02.280
and double the size of the
root PD volume so I can go in

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:05.244
and fix my spammy
log file, or fix

00:21:05.244 --> 00:21:07.410
whatever it is that's filling
up the root partition.

00:21:07.410 --> 00:21:11.360
Like it's just-- it's such
a convenient and powerful

00:21:11.360 --> 00:21:12.100
mechanism.

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:14.870
And it's not just related
to root volumes, obviously.

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:18.260
Like if you've got data
volumes that-- let's say

00:21:18.260 --> 00:21:21.710
you use GCS Connector to
pull a bunch of data from GCS

00:21:21.710 --> 00:21:26.830
to a local VM to a bunch of
VMs, PDs to do a Hadoop job.

00:21:26.830 --> 00:21:28.950
And you're writing
out the results,

00:21:28.950 --> 00:21:30.600
and all of a sudden
the job fails

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:34.270
because the data disk that you
were writing out to is full.

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:34.890
You know what?

00:21:34.890 --> 00:21:36.810
Like you just go
resize-- again, just

00:21:36.810 --> 00:21:40.520
go resize the disk and rerun
the job, and all of a sudden

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:42.510
you're good to go.

00:21:42.510 --> 00:21:43.760
You don't have to stop the VM.

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:46.124
You don't have to shut
everything down and detach it

00:21:46.124 --> 00:21:48.540
and resize it, or create a
bigger one and copy everything.

00:21:48.540 --> 00:21:50.944
It's just super, super powerful.

00:21:50.944 --> 00:21:51.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:21:51.860 --> 00:21:54.150
You could even consider
doing that through the API.

00:21:54.150 --> 00:21:57.580
So you don't need to even--
just click a single button.

00:21:57.580 --> 00:21:58.690
Just everything happens.

00:21:58.690 --> 00:21:59.440
It's just amazing.

00:21:59.440 --> 00:22:00.565
SCOTT VAN WOUDENBERG: Yeah.

00:22:00.565 --> 00:22:02.586
Set up a Stackdriver
alert that automatic--

00:22:02.586 --> 00:22:04.460
that triggers-- you
know, a Stackdriver alert

00:22:04.460 --> 00:22:08.460
that, when it gets 90% full,
call a cloud function that

00:22:08.460 --> 00:22:11.260
