00:22:41.144 --> 00:22:43.560
Like-- you want to talk a
little bit about live migrations

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:44.675
and how that works?

00:22:44.675 --> 00:22:46.050
SCOTT VAN WOUDENBERG:
Yeah, sure.

00:22:46.050 --> 00:22:50.330
So you know, the-- one of the
biggest challenges for a cloud

00:22:50.330 --> 00:22:54.960
provider is keeping the software
and hardware infrastructure

00:22:54.960 --> 00:22:59.590
that all the VMs run on,
keeping it up to date,

00:22:59.590 --> 00:23:01.480
keeping it patched,
secure, rolling out

00:23:01.480 --> 00:23:03.340
new versions of all
the different layers

00:23:03.340 --> 00:23:05.800
of that stack, et cetera.

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:08.940
And you know, if it's something
significant like upgrading

00:23:08.940 --> 00:23:11.710
the host kernel that is
running on the server

00:23:11.710 --> 00:23:14.280
that your VMs are running
on, like obviously that's

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:20.524
a very disruptive action
for [AUDIO OUT] or anything

00:23:20.524 --> 00:23:21.690
that's running on that host.

00:23:21.690 --> 00:23:24.700
You basically have to, like,
take everything off the host.

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:26.060
What we call drain.

00:23:26.060 --> 00:23:29.239
We have to drain the
host and do the upgrade,

00:23:29.239 --> 00:23:30.030
and then reboot it.

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:32.130
And it comes up
on the new kernel.

00:23:32.130 --> 00:23:36.110
That's all like-- and so
what-- with-- in that reality,

00:23:36.110 --> 00:23:38.207
in the reality of
maintaining these systems

00:23:38.207 --> 00:23:40.040
and keeping them all
up to date and patched,

00:23:40.040 --> 00:23:42.870
what we've done for GCE is
built this technology called

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:44.060
Live Migration.

00:23:44.060 --> 00:23:47.030
This is not like-- you know,
we didn't invent a technology.

00:23:47.030 --> 00:23:50.820
Like VMware has been doing their
own version of Live Migration--

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:53.490
they call it vMotion--
for many years.

00:23:53.490 --> 00:23:56.340
But we're, like, the first
public cloud hyperscale

00:23:56.340 --> 00:23:59.137
provider to do live
migration at the scale

00:23:59.137 --> 00:24:00.470
that we're doing live migration.

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:03.230
Like obviously, we run
lots and lots of VMs

00:24:03.230 --> 00:24:04.600
across many, many zones.

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:07.200
And we use Live
Migration quite a bit

00:24:07.200 --> 00:24:09.880
to make sure that all of
our underlying systems

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:14.422
are patched and updated, and
all without disrupting the VM.

00:24:14.422 --> 00:24:15.880
So what a live
migration does is it

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:19.390
basically-- your VM is
running on one host machine.

00:24:19.390 --> 00:24:21.860
And let's say we've got to
take that host machine offline

00:24:21.860 --> 00:24:23.960
to do a host kernel upgrade.

00:24:23.960 --> 00:24:28.440
So what we do is we trigger live
migrations for the VMs that are

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:29.930
running on that host machine.

00:24:29.930 --> 00:24:32.610
And what a live migration does
is, while the VM is running,

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:34.900
it starts copying
the state over-- it

00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:38.900
creates a new virtual machine
target on a different host,

00:24:38.900 --> 00:24:44.350
starts copying all of the state,
like RAM, the specification

00:24:44.350 --> 00:24:47.680
for the VM, the machine type,
all of the different PDs

00:24:47.680 --> 00:24:50.300
and things like that,
network connections.

00:24:50.300 --> 00:24:53.590
Basically, it creates an exact
mirror of that virtual machine

00:24:53.590 --> 00:24:55.030
on a different host.

00:24:55.030 --> 00:24:58.640
And then once we've copied
the majority of the state,

00:24:58.640 --> 00:25:02.320
we pause the source
VM briefly, and then

00:25:02.320 --> 00:25:04.850
unpause-- copy the rest
of the remaining state,

00:25:04.850 --> 00:25:06.450
and then unpause
it on the target.

00:25:06.450 --> 00:25:09.290
And now that VM is now running
on a different host machine

00:25:09.290 --> 00:25:13.230
without any impact-- well, with
minor performance and a very

00:25:13.230 --> 00:25:16.080
brief brownout-- a very
brief blackout window,

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:18.550
which is what we call that
pause period that it's actually

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:20.620
paused doing that
last transition in.

00:25:20.620 --> 00:25:23.220
And you know, that--
for most applications,

00:25:23.220 --> 00:25:26.460
that pause is
completely invisible.

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:29.460
And there is very little
performance degradation

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:32.020
for the majority of
our virtual machines.

00:25:32.020 --> 00:25:35.506
And so most customers just
don't even-- their workloads

00:25:35.506 --> 00:25:37.130
don't even notice
that we've done this.

00:25:37.130 --> 00:25:40.280
And it's very, very
powerful compared

00:25:40.280 --> 00:25:42.940
to the alternative, which is
essentially scheduling reboots

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:46.400
and, you know, oh, we need
to roll out a security patch

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:49.840
for like the latest Heartbleed
vulnerability or whatever.

00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:52.710
And so that required
our competition

00:25:52.710 --> 00:25:57.390
to reboot, like, a significant
chunk of their customers' VMs.

00:25:57.390 --> 00:26:00.090
And so there was like this
huge flurry of activity

00:26:00.090 --> 00:26:03.416
if you were a customer there
where you had to go basically

00:26:03.416 --> 00:26:04.790
figure out when
the best time you

00:26:04.790 --> 00:26:06.998
were going to be able to
reboot in the next few days,

00:26:06.998 --> 00:26:10.530
because the machines that were
running on-- that your VMs were

00:26:10.530 --> 00:26:11.880
running on were vulnerable.

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:14.250
And then Google
did the same thing.

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:15.770
We patched all of
our host machines.

00:26:15.770 --> 00:26:17.394
But we did it all
using Live Migration,

00:26:17.394 --> 00:26:19.200
so we didn't have to
require any reboots.

00:26:19.200 --> 00:26:20.158
