custom code, yeah,

00:32:36.920 --> 00:32:39.064
but you can get that up
and running, depending

00:32:39.064 --> 00:32:41.230
on how often you want to
be doing these full backups

00:32:41.230 --> 00:32:42.291
of this data.

00:32:42.291 --> 00:32:44.040
But yeah, then you can
take those backups,

00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:46.235
you can ingest
those into BigQuery,

00:32:46.235 --> 00:32:48.720
either through the admin
or through the API,

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:51.760
and then you can access
that data from BigQuery

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:54.145
and do your analytics and
all that sort of fun stuff.

00:32:54.145 --> 00:32:55.061
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:32:55.061 --> 00:32:56.630
Nothing really fancy, really.

00:32:56.630 --> 00:32:59.070
Just a bunch of
things that you need

00:32:59.070 --> 00:33:01.790
to plug one to each other, and
then everything just works.

00:33:01.790 --> 00:33:03.498
MARK MANDEL: Everything
should just work.

00:33:03.498 --> 00:33:06.240
So yeah, I mean like the
admin will create jobs for you

00:33:06.240 --> 00:33:08.840
and run through task queues
and all this sort of stuff

00:33:08.840 --> 00:33:10.630
in the background.

00:33:10.630 --> 00:33:13.920
It's worth noting, like I
said, it's not incremental.

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:14.860
It's full backups.

00:33:14.860 --> 00:33:17.635
So if, for example, you're
getting multiple kinds,

00:33:17.635 --> 00:33:19.260
they could be out of
sync a little bit.

00:33:19.260 --> 00:33:21.784
So you know, it's not going
to take a complete snapshot.

00:33:21.784 --> 00:33:23.700
So you sort of have to
take that into account.

00:33:23.700 --> 00:33:25.408
But for stuff you're
porting to BigQuery,

00:33:25.408 --> 00:33:27.242
I'm thinking that's
probably less important.

00:33:27.242 --> 00:33:28.157
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:33:28.157 --> 00:33:30.190
I guess that if you
really wanted something

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:33.360
that could do it weekly or
daily or something like that,

00:33:33.360 --> 00:33:36.310
you can always build your own
thing that could generate that.

00:33:36.310 --> 00:33:40.740
Like, it could just create
just a query of all the data

00:33:40.740 --> 00:33:42.920
that was created during
the last day or so.

00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:45.790
But you're going to have
to do more manual things.

00:33:45.790 --> 00:33:48.640
If you-- everything you're
going to do is the whole thing

00:33:48.640 --> 00:33:50.760
and just dump the whole
thing onto BigQuery,

00:33:50.760 --> 00:33:51.760
sounds like a good idea.

00:33:51.760 --> 00:33:53.370
MARK MANDEL: In some
cases, this also

00:33:53.370 --> 00:33:55.640
could push towards,
like, a good argument

00:33:55.640 --> 00:33:57.100
for using things like PubSub.

00:33:57.100 --> 00:33:57.975
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yes.

00:33:57.975 --> 00:34:00.490
MARK MANDEL: So that when
you go to store your data,

00:34:00.490 --> 00:34:02.520
it goes through your data, but
another subscriber maybe picks

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:04.450
that data up, shoves it
off into BigQuery as well,

00:34:04.450 --> 00:34:06.366
and then you can keep
that data alive in there

00:34:06.366 --> 00:34:08.889
and have multiple things do
stuff based on those actions.

00:34:08.889 --> 00:34:10.639
And PubSub is a really
great fit for that.

00:34:10.639 --> 00:34:12.601
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, absolutely.

